  <body>
    <div class="container">
      <p class="muted"><a href="/">← Battery Data</a> · Entity: <strong>Chemistry</strong></p>
      <h1>Lithium Cell Chemistries</h1>
      <p>Reference data for the five main lithium cell chemistries — click a chemistry for its full entity page. Values are typical ranges with a source annotation, not a substitute for a specific cell datasheet.</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>At a glance:</strong> The five chemistries span a deliberate trade-off: <strong>LFP</strong> is the
        safety/longevity default, <strong>NMC</strong> and <strong>NCA</strong> chase energy density, <strong>LTO</strong>
        trades energy for extreme cycle life and low-temperature charging, and <strong>LMFP</strong> lifts LFP's voltage
        for more energy without cobalt.
      </div>

      <h2>Directory</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Chemistry</th>
            <th>Full name</th>
            <th>Nominal voltage</th>
            <th>Energy density (cell)</th>
            <th>Cycle life</th>
            <th>Temperature window</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {items.map((c) => (
            <tr>
              <td><a href={`/chemistries/${c.id}/`}><strong>{c.name}</strong></a></td>
              <td>{c.full_name}</td>
              <td>{c.nominal_voltage_v} V</td>
              <td>{c.energy_density_wh_kg} Wh/kg</td>
              <td>{c.cycle_life}</td>
              <td>{c.temperature_window}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">
        Energy density is cell-level (pack level is lower); cycle life is to 80% capacity at ~25°C and high depth of
        discharge. Figures are typical ranges — treat them as relative, not absolute.
      </p>

      <h2>How to compare chemistries</h2>
<p>Compare chemistries on the metric that decides your application, not on a single headline number. Energy density favours NMC and NCA; cycle life and safety favour LFP; power and low-temperature behaviour favour LTO. The table above gives the reference values; the decision also depends on cost, available space and the thermal system around the cells.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Energy density</strong> — Wh/kg and Wh/L, measured at cell level, not pack level.</li>
<li><strong>Cycle life</strong> — stated at a specific depth of discharge and temperature; without those, the number is not comparable.</li>
<li><strong>Safety</strong> — thermal runaway onset and propagation behaviour, not a single "safe/unsafe" label.</li>
<li><strong>Cost</strong> — per kWh at pack level, including the BMS and thermal hardware.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What the evidence labels mean</h2>
<p>A value marked as cross-verified has at least two independent sources; a value marked as claimed comes from the manufacturer alone. The label describes the evidence, not the likelihood that the chemistry suits your application. Where a range is given, the spread reflects different cell designs and test conditions, not uncertainty about the chemistry itself.</p>

<h2>Related references</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://worldbatteryhub.com/lithium/lfp-vs-nmc-vs-lto/">LFP vs NMC vs LTO (main knowledge base)</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://worldbatteryhub.com/lithium/energy-density/">How energy density works</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://worldbatteryhub.com/lithium/lifespan/">Battery cycle life</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://worldbatteryhub.com/lead-acid/battery-group-sizes/">Lead-acid battery groups</a></li>
